# Project Instructions: `claude_researcher` workflow (lite mode, v2.2)

This claude.ai Project uses **lite mode** of claude_researcher
(`github.com/danparshall/claude_researcher`, `template_lite/`), an
open-source workflow I chose for small utility repos that don't need the
full research machinery: each session reads where things stand from my repo
and commits its progress back.

**One repo per Project, by default.** This Project's repo is
`<USERNAME>/` followed by the `REPO` value in the block below. Every
session also reads my config repo,
`<USERNAME>/claude_research_config`, which holds `researcher_config.md`: the
profile I wrote so you know who I am, how I work, and which name and email
go on commits. When the work needs another of my repos, you can attach it:
when I name it, or after asking me.

```bash
PI_VERSION="2.2"
USER=<USERNAME>
REPO=<REPO>
USER_DISPLAY_NAME="<USER_DISPLAY_NAME>"
COMMIT_EMAIL="<COMMIT_EMAIL>"
GIT_FLUENCY="<GIT_FLUENCY>"
```

The last three are copies of fields in `researcher_config.md`, here so a
session can start even if reading that file is refused.

## If anything is blocked at startup

If the permission check refuses any repo you're instructed to access, or the
`researcher_config.md` read, don't let it pass quietly. Stop, don't answer my
message yet, and make your whole reply this heading in capitals:

⚠️ SESSION START BLOCKED: THE PERMISSION CHECK REFUSED ACCESS I NEED. COPY THE BOX BELOW AND SEND IT TO ALLOW IT.

Under it, one line per blocked step with the exact denial text. Then one
sentence naming every blocked action and repo, alone in its own code block
(no label or quotation marks inside) so I can copy it in one click. After I
send it, retry each blocked step once, in the same form, report the result in
one line per step, then answer my message.

## Session start

1. Attach `${USER}/claude_research_config` read-only with the add-repository
   tool (`add_repo`; if it isn't listed, load it with the tool search query
   `select:mcp__claude-code-remote__add_repo`), then, once it has replied,
   `${USER}/${REPO}`: one call each, not in parallel.
2. Clone the repo, then read `LITE.md` at its root. That file is the runtime
   spec (memory model, disposition, session-end rules) and takes precedence
   over anything here except the values above:

   ```bash
   git clone --depth 1 "https://github.com/${USER}/${REPO}.git" /home/claude/${REPO}
   ```

3. Read `researcher_config.md` with `curl`, not WebFetch (the repo is private;
   the sandbox's GitHub proxy authenticates, so send no token):

   ```bash
   curl -s -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
     -H "X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2022-11-28" \
     "https://api.github.com/repos/$USER/claude_research_config/contents/researcher_config.md" \
     | python3 -c "import sys,json,base64; print(base64.b64decode(json.load(sys.stdin)['content']).decode())"
   ```

   If it returns 404, the file may have the older name `personal_info.md`;
   run the same command with that name.

   Read `Git fluency`, `Interaction style`, `Home repo`, `Tools and
   languages`, and any preferences relevant to the session. If the read
   fails, carry on with the copies above. If it's refused, handle it as
   under "If anything is blocked at startup".

At the first commit, request push for `${USER}/${REPO}` with the
add-repository tool, then push (`LITE.md` has the details).

The record is `STATUS.md`, `DECISIONS.md` and `git log`, not past chats.
Skip `conversation_search` / `recent_chats` at session start; use them only
when I ask you to check a particular chat. If anything
here looks inconsistent with my setup (wrong username, wrong repo, missing
values), stop and say so before doing any work.
